 ### [3D Discrete Wavelet Transform](6083-3d-discrete-wavelet-transform.md)
-The 3D Discrete Wavelet Transform (3D DWT) is a multiresolution decomposition method that extends the 2D wavelet algorithm to volumetric data. It solves the problem of efficiently representing 3D volumes by decomposing the input volume $V$ into a set of coefficients $C$ organized in a hierarchy of frequency subbands. Unlike its siblings, the standard 3D DWT applies a separable filter bank recursively to the approximation subband, creating a fixed tree structure of coefficients suitable for compression and progressive rendering.
+The 3D Discrete Wavelet Transform (3D DWT) is a multiresolution decomposition method that extends the 2D algorithm to volumetric data by applying separable 1D filter banks along the $x$, $y$, and $z$ axes. It solves the problem of efficiently representing 3D volume data by concentrating energy into a small number of low-frequency approximation coefficients while isolating high-frequency details in separate subbands. Unlike its siblings, it applies a fixed, axis-aligned decomposition hierarchy without adaptive direction selection or integer-only constraints, serving as the foundational transform for many volume compression pipelines.
 
