 ## Background
-The use of wavelets and pyramids for volume rendering compression emerged in the early 2000s as a response to the memory bandwidth limitations of GPUs. Key early works include those by Guthe et al. [S2, unofficial], who demonstrated hierarchical wavelet representations allowing 30:1 compression without noticeable artifacts, and Wetekam et al. [S1], who proposed octree-based multiresolution approaches. The Laplacian pyramid approach [S1] was adapted from image processing to allow on-the-fly reconstruction. More recent studies [S3] have refined decomposition structures (e.g., 5x3 vs 9x7 kernels) to balance compression efficiency with random-access performance for medical imaging.
+The use of wavelet transforms for volume rendering compression emerged in the early 2000s as a response to the memory bandwidth limitations of GPUs. Key early works include those by Guthe et al. [S2, unofficial], who demonstrated hierarchical wavelet representations allowing compression ratios of 30:1, and Wetekam et al. [S1], who proposed octree-based multiresolution approaches. The field has since diversified into specialized variants like Laplacian pyramids for on-the-fly rendering [S1][S2, unofficial][S3], biorthogonal filters for phase preservation [S2, unofficial], and video-coding-inspired slice compression [S1]. Recent developments include the application of these transforms to neural field representations via mask compression [S1].
 
